  "model_metadata": {
    "model_id": "model_smartgrid-input-20260629T085827Z-s101",
    "name": "OpenRE-Bench Integrated KAOS for SmartGrid",
    "description": "Deterministic protocol-aligned integrated GSN model.",
    "created_timestamp": "2026-06-29T08:59:58Z",
    "requirement_source": "Project: Smart Grid Energy Management Platform for Municipal Utility\n\n[System Context]\nThe system is a distributed energy management platform for a municipal utility serving 200,000 households. It integrates smart meters, renewable energy sources (solar panels, wind turbines), battery storage systems, and demand-response controllers. The system operates as a cyber-physical system with real-time monitoring and automated load balancing.\n\n[Core Functional Requirements]\n1. Real-Time Monitoring: The system shall collect meter readings from 200,000+ smart meters at 15-minute intervals and process the data stream in near real-time for grid stability analysis.\n2. Demand Response: The system shall automatically adjust load distribution based on real-time demand, renewable generation forecasts, and grid capacity constraints. Peak shaving algorithms must reduce peak demand by at least 15%.\n3. Renewable Integration: The system shall forecast solar and wind generation using weather data and historical patterns, and coordinate battery storage charge/discharge cycles to maximize renewable utilization.\n4. Billing & Analytics: The system shall support time-of-use pricing, net metering for prosumers, and provide consumption analytics dashboards for both utility operators and end consumers.\n\n[Non-Functional Constraints]\n1. Real-Time Performance: Grid stability decisions must be computed within 500ms. Meter data ingestion must sustain 50,000 messages/second during peak collection windows.\n2. Reliability: The control system must maintain operation during partial network failures. Failover to backup control centers must complete within 30 seconds. Data loss of meter readings must not exceed 0.01%.\n3. Safety: Automated load shedding must include safety interlocks to prevent equipment damage and ensure critical facilities (hospitals, emergency services) are never disconnected.\n4. Sustainability: The system must optimize for carbon footprint reduction. Reporting must include ISO 14001 environmental metrics. The platform itself must minimize computational energy consumption.\n5. Security: The SCADA/ICS communication channels must be protected against cyber attacks per NERC CIP standards. All remote access must use multi-factor authentication.\n6. Scalability: The platform must scale to support up to 500,000 endpoints without architecture changes.",
    "model_type": "GSN",
    "quality_attributes": [
      "Efficiency",
      "Responsibility",
      "Safety",
      "Sustainability",
      "Trustworthiness"
    ],
    "hierarchy_levels": [
      1,
      2
    ],
    "setting": "negotiation_integration_verification"
  },
